@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/CSharp/Views/Shared/CSharpLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Asynchronous & Parallel Programming";
}

<main id="tutorials" class="container py-5">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- LEFT SIDEBAR -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/CSharp/Views/Shared/CSharpTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- RIGHT CONTENT -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">

            <!-- MODULE INTRODUCTION -->
            <div class="card shadow-sm p-4 p-md-5 mb-4">

                <h2 class="h1 fw-bold text-dark mt-1 mb-4">
                    Module 7: Asynchronous &amp; Parallel Programming
                </h2>

                <p class="text-muted mb-4">
                    Learn how to build responsive and efficient C# applications
                    using Asynchronous Programming, Multithreading, and Parallel Processing.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info">
                    <strong>Module Objective:</strong>
                    Understand how asynchronous and parallel programming can improve
                    application responsiveness, performance, and resource utilization.
                </div>

            </div>


            <!-- ================= ASYNCHRONOUS PROGRAMMING ================= -->

            <div class="card shadow-sm p-4 p-md-5 mb-4" id="asynchronous-programming">

                <h2 class="h2 fw-bold text-dark mt-1 mb-4">
                    1. Asynchronous Programming
                </h2>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Asynchronous programming allows a program to start a task and
                    continue performing other work while waiting for that task to complete.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong>
                    Asynchronous programming allows long-running operations to execute
                    without blocking the main flow of the application.
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Why Do We Need Asynchronous Programming?
                </h4>

                <ul class="text-muted">
                    <li>Improves application responsiveness.</li>
                    <li>Prevents unnecessary blocking.</li>
                    <li>Works well with I/O operations.</li>
                    <li>Improves scalability in web applications.</li>
                    <li>Useful for database and API operations.</li>
                    <li>Allows applications to handle multiple operations efficiently.</li>
                </ul>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    async and await Keywords
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    The <strong>async</strong> keyword is used to define an asynchronous
                    method. The <strong>await</strong> keyword waits for an asynchronous
                    operation to complete without blocking the calling thread.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>static async Task GetDataAsync()
{
    await Task.Delay(2000);

    Console.WriteLine("Data received.");
}

static async Task Main()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Starting...");

    await GetDataAsync();

    Console.WriteLine("Completed.");
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Output</h5>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Starting...
Data received.
Completed.</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Task in C#
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Task represents an asynchronous operation.
                    It is commonly used with async and await.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>static async Task&lt;int&gt; CalculateAsync()
{
    await Task.Delay(1000);

    return 100;
}

static async Task Main()
{
    int result = await CalculateAsync();

    Console.WriteLine(result);
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Output</h5>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>100</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Async Method with Return Value
                </h4>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>static async Task&lt;string&gt; GetMessageAsync()
{
    await Task.Delay(1000);

    return "Hello from asynchronous method.";
}

static async Task Main()
{
    string message = await GetMessageAsync();

    Console.WriteLine(message);
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Output</h5>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Hello from asynchronous method.</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Running Multiple Async Operations
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Multiple asynchronous operations can be started and then awaited
                    together using Task.WhenAll().
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>static async Task TaskOne()
{
    await Task.Delay(1000);
    Console.WriteLine("Task One Completed");
}

static async Task TaskTwo()
{
    await Task.Delay(1000);
    Console.WriteLine("Task Two Completed");
}

static async Task Main()
{
    Task task1 = TaskOne();
    Task task2 = TaskTwo();

    await Task.WhenAll(task1, task2);

    Console.WriteLine("All Tasks Completed");
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Output</h5>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Task One Completed
Task Two Completed
All Tasks Completed</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Asynchronous Programming in ASP.NET Core
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    ASP.NET Core applications commonly use asynchronous methods
                    for database calls, API calls, file operations, and other
                    I/O-bound operations.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>public async Task&lt;IActionResult&gt; GetStudents()
{
    var students = await _studentService.GetStudentsAsync();

    return View(students);
}</code></pre>
                </div>


                <div class="alert alert-success">
                    <strong>CIIT Practical Point:</strong>
                    async and await are very important in ASP.NET Core development,
                    especially when working with Entity Framework Core, Web APIs,
                    HTTP requests, database queries, and file operations.
                </div>

            </div>


            <!-- ================= MULTITHREADING ================= -->

            <div class="card shadow-sm p-4 p-md-5 mb-4" id="multithreading">

                <h2 class="h2 fw-bold text-dark mt-1 mb-4">
                    2. Multithreading
                </h2>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Multithreading allows a program to execute multiple threads
                    concurrently. A thread represents an independent path of execution.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong>
                    Multithreading allows different tasks to execute concurrently
                    within an application.
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    What is a Thread?
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    A thread is the smallest unit of execution within a process.
                    An application can contain multiple threads.
                </p>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Creating a Thread
                </h4>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>using System.Threading;

class Program
{
    static void PrintMessage()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Thread is running.");
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        Thread thread = new Thread(PrintMessage);

        thread.Start();

        Console.WriteLine("Main thread is running.");
    }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    The exact order of the two output lines can vary because
                    both threads may execute concurrently.
                </p>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Thread with Parameter
                </h4>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>using System.Threading;

class Program
{
    static void PrintNumber(object number)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Number: " + number);
    }

    static void Main()
    {
        Thread thread =
            new Thread(PrintNumber);

        thread.Start(100);
    }
}</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Output</h5>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Number: 100</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Thread.Sleep()
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Thread.Sleep() pauses the current thread for a specified
                    amount of time.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>using System.Threading;

Console.WriteLine("Start");

Thread.Sleep(2000);

Console.WriteLine("End");</code></pre>
                </div>

                <h5 class="fw-bold">Output</h5>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Start
End</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Thread Synchronization
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    When multiple threads access shared data, synchronization
                    may be required to prevent unexpected results.
                </p>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    The <strong>lock</strong> keyword is commonly used to ensure
                    that only one thread accesses a critical section at a time.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>class Counter
{
    private int count = 0;

    private readonly object lockObject = new object();

    public void Increment()
    {
        lock (lockObject)
        {
            count++;
        }
    }

    public int GetCount()
    {
        return count;
    }
}</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Thread vs Task
                </h4>

                <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                    <table class="table table-bordered">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Feature</th>
                                <th>Thread</th>
                                <th>Task</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Abstraction</td>
                                <td>Lower-level</td>
                                <td>Higher-level</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Management</td>
                                <td>Manual</td>
                                <td>Managed by Task infrastructure</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Modern .NET Usage</td>
                                <td>Less common for general async work</td>
                                <td>Common</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Common Use</td>
                                <td>Specific threading scenarios</td>
                                <td>Async and parallel operations</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>


                <div class="alert alert-success">
                    <strong>CIIT Practical Point:</strong>
                    Multithreading concepts are useful when developing
                    background processing, concurrent services, desktop applications,
                    and performance-sensitive applications.
                </div>

            </div>


            <!-- ================= PARALLEL PROCESSING ================= -->

            <div class="card shadow-sm p-4 p-md-5 mb-4" id="parallel-processing">

                <h2 class="h2 fw-bold text-dark mt-1 mb-4">
                    3. Parallel Processing
                </h2>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Parallel processing divides work into multiple parts so that
                    several operations can execute at the same time.
                </p>

                <div class="alert alert-info">
                    <strong>Simple Definition:</strong>
                    Parallel processing is useful when independent operations
                    can be executed simultaneously to improve performance.
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Parallel.For
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Parallel.For executes iterations of a loop concurrently.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Parallel.For(1, 6, i =&gt;
{
    Console.WriteLine("Processing: " + i);
});</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    The order of the output is not guaranteed because
                    iterations may execute concurrently.
                </p>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Parallel.ForEach
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Parallel.ForEach is useful for processing each item in a collection
                    concurrently.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>List&lt;string&gt; courses =
    new List&lt;string&gt;
    {
        "C#",
        ".NET",
        "ASP.NET Core",
        "SQL"
    };

Parallel.ForEach(courses, course =&gt;
{
    Console.WriteLine("Processing: " + course);
});</code></pre>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Parallel.Invoke
                </h4>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    Parallel.Invoke executes multiple independent actions in parallel.
                </p>

                <div class="code-block mb-4 shadow-sm">
                    <div class="code-lang-tag">C#</div>
                    <pre class="mb-0"><code>Parallel.Invoke(
    () =&gt; Console.WriteLine("Task 1"),
    () =&gt; Console.WriteLine("Task 2"),
    () =&gt; Console.WriteLine("Task 3")
);</code></pre>
                </div>

                <p class="text-muted">
                    The order in which the tasks print can vary.
                </p>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    Async vs Multithreading vs Parallel Processing
                </h4>

                <div class="table-responsive mb-4">
                    <table class="table table-bordered">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>Concept</th>
                                <th>Main Purpose</th>
                                <th>Common Example</th>
                            </tr>
                        </thead>
                        <tbody>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Asynchronous Programming</td>
                                <td>Non-blocking operations</td>
                                <td>API or database call</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Multithreading</td>
                                <td>Concurrent execution using threads</td>
                                <td>Background processing</td>
                            </tr>
                            <tr>
                                <td>Parallel Processing</td>
                                <td>Execute independent work simultaneously</td>
                                <td>Large data processing</td>
                            </tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>


                <h4 class="fw-bold text-dark mt-4 mb-3">
                    When Should We Use Parallel Processing?
                </h4>

                <ul class="text-muted">
                    <li>Large data processing.</li>
                    <li>Independent calculations.</li>
                    <li>Image processing.</li>
                    <li>Batch processing.</li>
                    <li>CPU-intensive operations.</li>
                    <li>Operations that can safely execute independently.</li>
                </ul>


                <div class="alert alert-warning">
                    <strong>Important:</strong>
                    Parallel processing is not automatically faster for every problem.
                    For small operations, the overhead of parallel execution may provide
                    little or no performance benefit.
                </div>


                <div class="alert alert-success">
                    <strong>CIIT Practical Point:</strong>
                    Parallel processing is useful for CPU-intensive workloads,
                    batch operations, data processing, and applications where
                    independent work can safely execute concurrently.
                </div>

            </div>


